Levels marking

| **Level 1 (1–3 marks)** | **Level 2 (4–6 marks)** | **Level 3 (7 marks)** |
|---|---|---|
| Statements including limited detail which describe the different methods which are used to develop tourism without destroying the natural environment | Uses named example. More developed statements which describe the different methods which are used to develop tourism without destroying the natural environment. (NB Max 5 if no named or inappropriate example) | Uses named example. Comprehensive and accurate statements which describe the different methods which are used to develop tourism without destroying the natural environment, including some place references. |

**Content Guide:** 
<u>Examples include:</u>

Creation of National Parks; 
Limit numbers of tourists; 
Charge entrance fees; 
Conservation; 
Fence sensitive areas; 
Ban hunting; 
Only allow people access to an area with a guide; 
Educate people about caring for the environment; 
Develop ecotourism etc.

NB: Development is of methods, not impact on environment.

- *Development marks are given for the method of management, and not the effect of tourism on the environment*

<u>Place specific examples include:</u>

Locational details - ** ***Bhutan, in southern Asia, is a landlocked, small and remote kingdom in the Himalayas between China in the north and India to the south.* 
Specific details of strategies -  *Ecotourism is a low-impact and small-scale, environmentally friendly tourism method that allows a small number of people to visit fragile and unspoilt areas of the environment. Ecotourism's main goal is for visitors to leave a small carbon footprint, to the benefit of the community and environment. 74% of Bhutan is forest, with 51% designated as a protected area. Bhutan has committed to keeping 60% of its land area under forest cover forever.  All tourists to Bhutan are expected to pay a daily 'Sustainable Development Fee' of US\$200, which pays for important developmental projects and offsetting the country's tourism carbon footprint. Bhutan restricts the number of tourists to its borders, with the government's ethos of 'high value, low volume' tourism, that creates a sense of exclusivity with only 5000 foreign visitors per year, this controls the volume and impact of tourism... *

